      "text": "Given a circle with a center on the x-axis, let the center be at \\((h, 0)\\). Since the circle passes through the points \\((0, 4)\\) and \\((1, 3)\\), the distance from the center \\((h, 0)\\) to each of these points must be equal to the radius \\(r\\).\n\nThe distance formula between two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the distance from \\((h, 0)\\) to \\((0, 4)\\):\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{(0 - h)^2 + (4 - 0)^2} = \\sqrt{h^2 + 16}\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the distance from \\((h, 0)\\) to \\((1, 3)\\):\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{(1 - h)^2 + (3 - 0)^2} = \\sqrt{(1 - h)^2 + 9}\n\\]\n\nSince both expressions equal the radius \\(r\\), we set them equal to each other:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{h^2 + 16} = \\sqrt{(1 - h)^2 + 9}\n\\]\n\nSquare both sides to eliminate the square roots:\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = (1 - h)^2 + 9\n\\]\n\nExpand the right-hand side:\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = 1 - 2h + h^2 + 9\n\\]\n\nSimplify the equation:\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = h^2 - 2h + 10\n\\]\n\nSubtract \\(h^2\\) from both sides:\n\\[\n16 = -2h + 10\n\\]\n\nRearrange to solve for \\(h\\):\n\\[\n16 - 10 = -2h\n\\]\n\\[\n6 = -2h\n\\]\n\\[\nh = -3\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute \\(h = -3\\) back into the expression for the radius:\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{(-3)^2 + 16} = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = \\sqrt{25} = 5\n\\]\n\nThus, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
