      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the radius of the circle, we need to find the coordinates of its center and use the distance formula. The center of the circle lies on the x-axis, so its y-coordinate is 0. Let's denote the center of the circle as \\((h, 0)\\).\n\nSince the points \\((0, 4)\\) and \\((1, 3)\\) lie on the circle, the distance from the center \\((h, 0)\\) to each of these points is the same and equals the radius \\(r\\) of the circle. We can use the distance formula to set up the following equations for the distances from the center to the points \\((0, 4)\\) and \\((1, 3)\\):\n\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{(h - 0)^2 + (0 - 4)^2} = \\sqrt{h^2 + 16}\n\\]\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + (0 - 3)^2} = \\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + 9}\n\\]\n\nSince both expressions are equal to \\(r\\), we can set them equal to each other:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{h^2 + 16} = \\sqrt{(h - 1)^2 + 9}\n\\]\n\nSquaring both sides to eliminate the square roots, we get:\n\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = (h - 1)^2 + 9\n\\]\n\nExpanding the right side of the equation:\n\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = h^2 - 2h + 1 + 9\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the right side:\n\n\\[\nh^2 + 16 = h^2 - 2h + 10\n\\]\n\nSubtracting \\(h^2\\) from both sides:\n\n\\[\n16 = -2h + 10\n\\]\n\nSubtracting 10 from both sides:\n\n\\[\n6 = -2h\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(h\\):\n\n\\[\nh = -3\n\\]\n\nNow that we have the x-coordinate of the center, we can find the radius \\(r\\) by substituting \\(h = -3\\) back into one of the distance formulas. Using the distance from the center to the point \\((0, 4)\\):\n\n\\[\nr = \\sqrt{(-3 - 0)^2 + (0 - 4)^2} = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = \\sqrt{25} = 5\n\\]\n\nThus, the radius of the circle is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
